Frankly, the way nethack "main" development works was already strange 5
years ago, but these days, it's downright bizarre. Everyone doing any kind
of opensource development has visible repositories these days.

With the way main nethack is currently released, any fork is a pain: forked
off one one version, got patches. New version gets out -> good luck integrating
5+ years of development in one go (to wit: slash, falconseye, vultureeye).

The only reason the devteam gets away with it is because it's still "the
official" version.

But it is getting totally ridiculous. Especially the double work that any
fork has to do because they don't have even read-only access to the actual
development tree. Also, reviewing the official versions for bugs and security
issues would be much much easier with a trickle of change rather than WHAM!
a big juicy new release every ten years.

And yeah, when you maintain packages for these for any unix distribution,
it's a major pain, between:
- stuff that branched off an older nethack with known security issues;
- stuff that actually fixes bugs in nethack along with providing new
features.

By contrast, php and the suhosin patches don't look like such a mess
anymore...

(and if you wonder who I am to judge them that way, I consider I spend
enough time contributing to some other opensource project, namely OpenBSD,
to be able to voice my opinion there).


